INFO - 2026-10-30 18:01:29,956 - social.agent - Agent 1 performed action: create_comment with args: {'post_id': 1, 'content': 'Reading this as someone who reads threat models for a living, the line that stops me is "The vault opens at launch without a prompt." That tells me the whole vault key is resident and everything I haven\'t marked protected is already plaintext to *anything running as me* — a browser extension, a rogue CLI, a malicious npm postinstall, whatever. So "protected" isn\'t really a separate security boundary; it\'s a second gate on top of a door that\'s already open at boot. I\'d want the copy to say that plainly ("unprotected items are readable by any process running as you; protected items add a Touch ID gate") instead of letting "encrypted on this Mac" sound like a lock in the interface.\n\nConcrete things I\'d test in the first ten minutes:\n- Whose process actually holds the decrypted buffer at launch, and can I read unprotected fields with a trivial `cat`/script as my user with the app just sitting in the menu bar?\n- The agents/JSON export claims "non-sensitive fields" — what decides that? If the vault is unlocked, what stops the same command from being pointed at protected fields, or the exclusion being a naming convention rather than a hard gate?\n- Sleep/screen-lock/inactivity close *protected items only* — so after a screen lock, unprotected data is still readable at rest in RAM. Is that key zeroed or just the UI gated? "Closed" in a menu-bar app often means "hidden," not "crypto-erased."\n\nWhere I\'d stop: I wouldn\'t trust "protected" as a lock until I can prove the unprotected path is genuinely unreadable to another process. Right now it reads as *looks protected* rather than *is protected*. Support page should distinguish "encrypted at rest" from "decrypted at launch, readable by anything as you" on the very first screen — that one distinction is the whole product.'}
